For Hermitian X, coeff(X)_mu=Tr(P_mu X)/4 is real and has Euclidean norm
||X||_F/2<=||X||_op. Define

    G(a)=coeff(Im(U0^dagger F(a))),
    Im M=(M-M^dagger)/(2i).

G(a0)=0. Since U0^dagger partial_j F is skew-Hermitian, its Jacobian is

    A_mu,j=Tr[P_mu(-i U0^dagger partial_j F)]/4.             (1)

The sign is minus i: applying a positive scalar makes a negative phase.
The code preserves this sign and the center coordinate.

Duhamel expansion with unitary intervening factors gives

    ||partial_j F||<=c=2 integral_0^1 psi=16/15,
    ||partial_j partial_k F||<=c^2.

For j=k the second derivative contains two ordered insertions. Their factor
2 cancels the half-square integration domain, giving exactly the same bound.
For real unit directions u,v, summing selected derivatives bounds the second
directional derivative by c^2||u||_1||v||_1<=16c^2. Taking Im and normalized
Pauli coordinates cannot increase that bound. Thus DG is L-Lipschitz on the
convex parameter ball, L=16c^2=4096/225.

The exact inverse-residual calculation in section 6 proves ||A^-1||<=126.
Using the weaker ||A^-1||<=1/s=252, choose the t,r above. They obey

    t<=1/8,       Lt/s<=1/2,       4ct<=1/4,
    rho=st/2,    r=rho/2<=1/8.

For ||y||<=rho, the map

    Phi_y(x)=A^-1[y-G(a0+x)+Ax]

has Lipschitz constant <=Lt/s<=1/2 on the closed radius-t ball and maps it
within radius t/2+Lt^2/(2s)<=3t/4. Banach's contraction theorem supplies an
interior preimage. Also ||F(a0+x)-U0||<=4ct<=1/4.

For a unitary V with ||V-U0||<r, its imaginary coordinate y has norm <rho.
Both U0^dagger F and U0^dagger V have positive definite real Hermitian part.
